{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1445567-lycanthropic-flowers","id":1445567,"title":"lycanthropic flowers","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-04-23T16:25:11-07:00","id":1445567,"title":"lycanthropic flowers","content":"I like to include as much folklore and legends concerning werewolves as I can in my games. It is very easy for WtA to diverge into real high fantasy that departs from its actual source material. I find incorporating what folklore I can helps ground the game so players can keep in mind they are playing werewolves and not some D&amp;D style fantasy race.<br /> <br /> One aspect I've been reading up on is the subject of lycanthropic flowers \u2013 flowers that in some way cause people to change into werewolves, or are somehow tied to werewolves. You can find a lot about the subject online. Such flowers are included on many lists of \u201chow to become a werewolf.\u201d<br /> <br /> I wanted to know if there was some kind of real world basis for this, or if it was just fakelore like many of the \u201cfacts\u201d created by the Universal horror films. I don't mind fakelore. That's part of the game too as it's what people recognize as being part of \u201cwerewolf lore.\u201d There's a lot of fakelore about vampires too that get handled in various ways in VtM. But I wanted to know what was the basis of this part of the legend before I decided how to use it within the game.<br /> <br /> After some thorough review, I believe lycanthropic flowers are an invention of early 20th century British storyteller Elliot O'Donnell. O'Donnell wrote many stories and books, often involving the supernatural, including the book Werewolves. He presented his stories in Werewolves as actual local legends and stories, but these actually seem to be wholly invented fictions by him. I can't find any antecedents to them. But they're often found in a lot of subsequent anthologies taken at face value. They're even mentioned a lot in RPG material! But that's good enough for me. Mystery solved.<br /> <br /> So what does O'Donnell's book tell us about them? These fictional flowers are known to turn people into werewolves, or at least mysteriously attract them.<br /> <br /> They have a variety of colors, with white, yellow, blue, red, and orange being mentioned. All are particularly vivid and brilliant. Another common characteristic is their sweet, nauseating odor similar to decaying animal and vegetable matter. Their sap is offensively thick, white, and sticky. Flowers are found in a variety of desolate locations \u2013 many mountain ranges and swamps or other wetlands.<br /> <br /> They have several shapes, but are compared to sunflowers, ox-eye daisies, and snapdragons. These tend to be big, pulpy, noticeable flowers. While not mentioned as lycanthropic flowers, O'Donnell says certain flowers attract werewolves and the examples he gives are lilies-of-the-valley, marigolds, and azaleas. These real flowers share similar appearance to O'Donnell's fictional ones, though I think lack the deathly odor that distinguishes their fictional counterparts.<br /> <br /> How can we use these fictional flowers in WtA to any benefit? We obviously need to modify them to fit within the game's lore. Here is what I would use. But feel free to add your own ideas.<ul><li>They are the form of some Englings who sometimes materialize in the Realm.</li> <li>When consumed, the rush of Gnosis helps pre-Change cubs turn into Garou.</li> <li>They are usually found in places where the Gauntlet is low; particularly near caerns, but any remote wilderness is also possible. I would not place such flowers anywhere near civilization \u2013 its not found at urban or rural caerns, just in the remote reaches of the wilderness.</li> <li>adolescent pre-Change Garou sometimes find themselves drawn to remote locations to find these flowers and strangely compelled to pluck them, eat them, smell them, etc.</li> <li>Engling description says they are jagglings, but perhaps these are specialized gaffling versions.</li> <li>The Engling might manifest as a large number of flowers, though only provides 1 Gnosis total. After the first person eats one and consumes the Gnosis, any remaining flowers don't provide anything.</li> <li>After being plucked, these flowers should fade away quickly through accelerated decomposition. This is to minimize the amount of scientific research that can be attempted on them. They should remain outside the realm of real scientific botany and remain in cryptobotany. They also never propagate in the wild since they are not actual flowers with seeds, etc. They only take the form of flowers, though any site which hosts such manifested spirits are likely to see new Englings arrive and manifest in the Realm again, creating the appearance of renewal.</li> </ul>These flowers might also be useful in dealing with Lost Cubs and Lunatics with certain affects. The flowers then could become a plot coupon you need to achieve certain story goals \u2013 alleviating Harano, helping a Lunatic, bringing Rage under control (the Beast Within), changing a pre-Change Metis into a more appropriate form, or something else dealing with the Garou condition.<br /> <br /> Whether any of the different descriptions of these flowers indicate different flowers having different properties and uses, is up to the ST. This could lead to sending PCs out on a quest to find a specific kind of flower to achieve a certain end.<br /> <br /> I am interested in hearing what others would do this with this concept, or any other thoughts they have on them. Would they make them real actual flowers in the game setting, use them as talens,or do something else?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-23T18:29:17-07:00","id":1445583,"title":"","content":"You could go the route of requiring the rite of spirit awakening for a mystical effect. That was the case the last time the game discussed the issue of botanical use by Garou, all the way back in Axis Mundi, I believe. At that point, the effect is a function of the plant-spirit in question.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-25T10:49:58-07:00","id":1445846,"title":"","content":"You could also make them spirits of the Wyld. Their effects could be tied to changing those who consume them or such into agents of the Wyld, gorgons in effect. In face, I have been toying with the possibility that the Wyld would use such tactics to create its own tribe of Garou. Why bring others to its side, when it can just make its own soldiers? And why stop at wolves? I feel that the Wyld would blend any two, or more, being into a transforming whole. Back to topic, the flowers could be actual Gorgons that could &quot;infect&quot; another when consumed by a potential Garou. The flower overrides any such original possibilities, forming a new Garou on the side of the Wyld. With this, as well as converts from other tribes, the Wyld could make its own version of the BSD. A tribe of this serving the Wyld in all of its dynamic possibilities.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>THWarted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/17984-toby-weingarten","name":"Toby Weingarten","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-25T18:53:30-07:00","id":1445900,"title":"","content":"The problem with being Wyld-spirits is that the plant-based effects described in the OP feel Gaian, and the Wyld isn't, for all the tangling that the two concepts had in the earliest books. Also, the Garou fall a bit too heavy on the Weaver-side of things for the Wyld to make an attempt like that; even the Black Furies know better than to let Thresholds in caerns grow, and the Red Talons spend a bit too much time stewing in the desire to murder humans as horribly as possible and desiring to be just wolves. The tribes as a whole hide behind the Veil, and they tend to want to do things quietly and in measured dosages.<br /> <br /> The Ratkin, on the other hand, were driven out, their culture was gutted, and they have very little in the way of a coherent center, especially when their purpose was &quot;keep human numbers under control&quot;. So, howling into the madness they have often gone.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-25T20:39:11-07:00","id":1445916,"title":"","content":"I think the Wyld would be an interesting route to take. Not everything of the Wyld is gonzo &quot;change whatever it comes into contact with&quot; transmogrifiers. There are more subdued Wyld spirits like the Serpentines, which are healing serpents which I assume represent the healing snakes of Asclepius among many other worldwide myths and legends.<br /> <br /> And in Garou theology, the Fae are supposed to be creatures of the Wyld. And I think some of the concepts in Changeling might come into play here. Maybe these lycanthropic flowers would be plants that Changelings would consider to be sources of Dross and provide Glamour. So their presence could indicate that there is a Fount, Glade or other natural Freehold nearby. Maybe these areas are places where Garou and Fae collaborate to protect. Therefore, Fianna or packs of Stag might want to watch over such places.<br /> <br /> It would also open up more options for the ST in terms of effects.<br /> <br /> So while I personally wouldn't make this strongly of the Wyld, I do think a more subtle manifestation of it (like the Fae are supposed to be) would make an intriguing option.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 04-25-2021, 11:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-26T15:38:30-07:00","id":1445998,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1445567-lycanthropic-flowers?p=1445916#post1445916\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the Wyld would be an interesting route to take. Not everything of the Wyld is gonzo &quot;change whatever it comes into contact with&quot; transmogrifiers. There are more subdued Wyld spirits like the Serpentines, which are healing serpents which I assume represent the healing snakes of Asclepius among many other worldwide myths and legends.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's also a rite that turns a human you don't like into a crazed Crinos every full moon so that they kill those closest to them. So, limited transformations from odd sources can also be Gaian, which plants will be.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And in Garou theology, the Fae are supposed to be creatures of the Wyld. And I think some of the concepts in Changeling might come into play here. Maybe these lycanthropic flowers would be plants that Changelings would consider to be sources of Dross and provide Glamour. So their presence could indicate that there is a Fount, Glade or other natural Freehold nearby. Maybe these areas are places where Garou and Fae collaborate to protect. Therefore, Fianna or packs of Stag might want to watch over such places. </div> </div> </div>Nonetheless, fae doings are from a different game. Have been since 1995. How much of the Wyld they are is also up for debate, since they occupy a different region of weird shit and the link that they might have with it is quite possibly more of a sympathy thing than a hard &quot;they came from it&quot; thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It would also open up more options for the ST in terms of effects.<br /> <br /> So while I personally wouldn't make this strongly of the Wyld, I do think a more subtle manifestation of it (like the Fae are supposed to be) would make an intriguing option. </div> </div> </div>The setting has shifted to anything strictly Triatic being, at best, playing with fire. Also, the Wyld, as a matter of lore, avoids humanity, so it might not be the best choice to turn humans into werewolves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-09T15:03:57-07:00","id":1448071,"title":"","content":"After much thought and borrowing some books from a friend, I think I figured out the most elegant way to include this using existing rules. The Book of the Wyld has a section on &quot;Wyld Fetishes&quot; which aren't actual fetishes. Instead, they are benefits or bounties bestowed by the Wyld to the Garou. They can take many forms, but a typical one is for a place of the Wyld to empower local animal or plant life. Such gifts are attuned to only the Garou they are meant for, and can't be used by anyone else. I think the concept fits perfectly for my game needs.<br /> <br /> Lycanthropic flowers are a Wyld fetish that manifests as a flower found at remote places of the Wyld. These upswellings of lycanthropic flowers are brief, and always vanish upon a return visit. They are only found by solitary Garou, especially pre-Change ones whose change was delayed or forestalled for whatever reason. Occasionally such flowers appear to other Garou are require such blessings. The benefits of plucking such flowers and wearing or consuming them fits the reason the Wyld fetish has manifested to that Garou. Forcing a First Change is a common one, but other benefits have included curing Lunatics, temporarily alleviating Harano, or temporarily suppressing the Curse.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}